DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       This program is part of NNeettppbbmm(1).

       jjppeeggttooppnnmm converts JFIF images to PPM or PGM images.

       By default, jjppeeggttooppnnmm expects the input stream to contain one JFIF  im‐
       age and produces one PGM or PPM image as output.  It fails if the input
       stream is empty.

       But with the --mmuullttiippllee option, jjppeeggttooppnnmm reads JFIF images sequentially
       from  the  input  stream  and writes one PPM or PGM image to the output
       stream for each JFIF input.  If the input stream is empty,  so  is  the
       output.

       The  input  stream is the _f_i_l_e_n_a_m_e you specify or, if you don't specify
       _f_i_l_e_n_a_m_e, Standard Input.  The output stream is Standard Output.

       If a JFIF input image is of the grayscale variety, jjppeeggttooppnnmm  generates
       a PGM image.  Otherwise, it generates a PPM image.

       Before Netpbm 10.11 (October 2002), jjppeeggttooppnnmm did not have the multiple
       image stream capability.  From 10.11 through 10.22, Netpbm  always  be‐
       haved  as if you specified --mmuullttiippllee.  Starting with Netpbm 10.23 (July
       2004), Netpbm's default behavior went back to  the  pre-10.11  behavior
       and  the  new --mmuullttiippllee option selected the 10.12 behavior.  The reason
       for the reversion was that there were discovered  in  the  world  files
       that  contain JFIF images followed by something other than another JFIF
       image.  The producers of these files expect them to work with any  JFIF
       interpreter  because  most JFIF interpreters just stop reading the file
       after the first JFIF image.

       jjppeeggttooppnnmm uses the Independent JPEG Group's JPEG library  to  interpret
       the  input  file.  See hhttttpp::////wwwwww..iijjgg..oorrgg ⟨⟨hhttttpp::////wwwwww..iijjgg..oorrgg⟩⟩  for in‐
       formation on the library.

       "JFIF" is the correct name for  the  image  format  commonly  known  as
       "JPEG."  Strictly speaking, JPEG is a method of compression.  The image
       format using JPEG compression that is by far the most common  is  JFIF.
       There is also a subformat of TIFF that uses JPEG compression.

       EXIF  is  an  image  format that is a subformat of JFIF (to wit, a JFIF
       file that contains an EXIF header as an APP1 marker).   jjppeeggttooppnnmm  han‐
       dles EXIF.

       JFIF  files  can  have  either 8 bits per sample or 12 bits per sample.
       The 8 bit variety is by far the most common.  There are two versions of
       the  IJG  JPEG library.  One reads only 8 bit files and the other reads
       only 12 bit files.  You must link the  appropriate  one  of  these  li‐
       braries  with jjppeeggttooppnnmm.  Ordinarily, this means the library is in your
       shared library search path when you run jjppeeggttooppnnmm.

       jjppeeggttooppnnmm generates output with either one byte or two bytes per sample
       depending  on  whether  the JFIF input has either 8 bits or 12 bits per
       sample.  You can use ppaammddeepptthh to reduce a two-byte-per-sample file to a
       one-byte-per-sample file if you need to.

       If  the JFIF file uses the CMYK or YCCK color space, the input does not
       actually contain enough information to know what color each  pixel  is.
       To know what color a pixel is, one would have to know the properties of
       the inks to which the color space  refers.   jjppeeggttooppnnmm  interprets  the
       colors  using  the common transformation which assumes all the inks are
       simply subtractive and linear.

       See the jjppeeggttooppnnmm mmaannuuaall(1) for information on how images lose  quality
       when you convert to and from JFIF.


OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       In  addition  to  the options common to all programs based on libnetpbm
       (most notably --qquuiieett, see
        Common Options ⟨index.html#commonoptions⟩ ), jjppeeggttooppnnmm recognizes  the
       following command line options:

       The options are only for advanced users.



       --ddcctt iinntt
              Use integer DCT method (default).


       --ddcctt ffaasstt
              Use fast integer DCT (less accurate).


       --ddcctt ffllooaatt
              Use  floating-point  DCT  method.   The  float  method  is  very
              slightly more accurate than the int method, but is  much  slower
              unless your machine has very fast floating-point hardware.  Also
              note that results of the floating-point method may vary slightly
              across  machines, while the integer methods should give the same
              results everywhere.  The fast integer method is much less  accu‐
              rate than the other two.


       --nnoossmmooootthh
              Use a faster, lower-quality upsampling routine.

       --mmaaxxmmeemmoorryy _N
              Set  limit  on the amount of memory jjppeeggttooppnnmm uses in processing
              large images.  Value is in thousands of bytes,  or  millions  of
              bytes if "M" is suffixed to the number.  For example, --mmaaxxmmeemmoorryy
              44mm selects 4000000 bytes.  If jjppeeggttooppnnmm  needs  more  space,  it
              uses temporary files.


       --aaddoobbee

       --nnoottaaddoobbee
              There  are  two variations on the CMYK (and likewise YCCK) color
              space that may be used in the JFIF input.  In the normal one,  a
              zero  value for a color components indicates absence of ink.  In
              the other, a zero value means the  maximum  ink  coverage.   The
              latter  is  used  by Adobe Photoshop when it creates a bare JFIF
              output file (but not when it creates JFIF output as part of  En‐
              capsulated Postscript output).

              These  options  tell jjppeeggttooppnnmm which version of the CMYK or YCCK
              color space the image uses.  If you specify  neither,  jjppeeggttooppnnmm
              tries  to  figure it out on its own.  In the present version, it
              doesn't try very hard at all: It just assumes the Photoshop ver‐
              sion,  since  Photoshop  and  its  emulators seem to be the main
              source of CMYK and YCCK images.  But with experience of use, fu‐
              ture versions might be more sophisticated.

              If  the  JFIF  image  does not indicate that it is CMYK or YCCK,
              these options have no effect.

              If you don't use the right one of these options, the symptom  is
              output that looks like a negative.


       --dduummppeexxiiff
              Print  the  interpreted contents of any Exif header in the input
              file to the Standard Error file.  Similar to the  program  jjhheeaadd
              (not part of the Netpbm package).

              This option was added in Netpbm 9.19 (September 2001).


       --eexxiiff==_f_i_l_e_s_p_e_c
              Extract the contents of the EXIF header from the input image and
              write it to the file _f_i_l_e_s_p_e_c.  _f_i_l_e_s_p_e_c=--  means  write  it  to
              Standard  Output.   When  you  write the EXIF header to Standard
              Output, jjppeeggttooppnnmm does not output the converted image (which  is
              what normally would go to Standard Output) at all.

              jjppeeggttooppnnmm  writes the contents of the EXIF header byte-for-byte,
              starting with the two byte length field (which  length  includes
              those two bytes).

              You can use this file as input to ppnnmmttoojjppeegg to insert an identi‐
              cal EXIF header into a new JFIF image.

              If there is no EXIF header, jjppeeggttooppnnmm writes two bytes of binary
              zero and nothing else.

              An  EXIF  header takes the form of a JFIF APP1 marker.  Only the
              first such marker within the JFIF header counts.

              This option was added in Netpbm 9.19 (September 2001).


       --mmuullttiippllee
              Read multiple JFIF images sequentially from  the  input  stream.
              See Description section ⟨#description⟩  for details.

              This option was new in Netpbm 10.23 (July 2004).


       --rreeppaaiirr
              If  the  JFIF input is invalid, try to salvage whatever informa‐
              tion is there and produce a valid PNM image as output.

              Without this option, some  invalid  input  causes  jjppeeggttooppnnmm  to
              fail,  and  what  output it produces is undefined.  With --rreeppaaiirr
              such invalid input causes jjppeeggttooppnnmm to succeed instead.

              But note that there are some forms of invalid input that  always
              cause  jjppeeggttooppnnmm to fail and others that always cause it to sal‐
              vage image information and succeed.

              One particular case where --rreeppaaiirr makes a difference is the com‐
              mon  case  that  the  file is truncated somewhere after the JFIF
              header.  Without --rreeppaaiirr, that always  causes  a  failure;  with
              --rreeppaaiirr it always causes success.  Because the image information
              is laid out generally top to bottom in the JFIF stream, the  im‐
              age  jjppeeggttooppnnmm  produces  in this case has the proper image con‐
              tents at the top, but the bottom is padded with gray.

              This option was new in  Netpbm  10.38.0  (March  2007).   Before
              that, jjppeeggttooppnnmm always fails in the cases in question.



       --ccoommmmeennttss
              Print any comments in the input file to the Standard Error file.


       --vveerrbboossee
              Print details about the conversion to the Standard Error file.


       --ttrraacceelleevveell _n
              Turn  on the JPEG library's trace messages to the Standard Error
              file.  A higher value of _n gets more trace  information.   --vveerr‐‐
              bboossee implies a trace level of at least 1.




EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
       This  example  converts the color JFIF file foo.jpg to a PPM file named
       foo.ppm:

           jpegtopnm foo.jpg >foo.ppm



HHIINNTTSS
       You can use ppnnmmqquuaanntt to color quantize the result, i.e. to  reduce  the
       number  of  distinct  colors in the image.  In fact, you may have to if
       you want to convert the PPM file to certain other  formats.   ppppmmddiitthheerr
       Does a more sophisticated quantization.

       Use ppaammssccaallee to change the dimensions of the resulting image.

       Use ppppmmttooppggmm  to convert a color JFIF file to a grayscale PGM file.

       You can easily use these converters together.  E.g.:

           jpegtopnm foo.jpg | ppmtopgm | pamscale .25 >foo.pgm


       --ddcctt ffaasstt and/or --nnoossmmooootthh gain speed at a small sacrifice in quality.

       If  you are fortunate enough to have very fast floating point hardware,
       --ddcctt ffllooaatt may be even faster than --ddcctt ffaasstt.   But  on  most  machines
       --ddcctt ffllooaatt is slower than --ddcctt iinntt; in this case it is not worth using,
       because its theoretical accuracy advantage is too small to be  signifi‐
       cant in practice.

       Another  program,  ddjjppeegg, is similar.  ddjjppeegg is maintained by the Inde‐
       pendent JPEG Group and packaged with the JPEG library  which  jjppeeggttooppnnmm
       uses  for all its JPEG work.  Because of that, you may expect it to ex‐
       ploit more current JPEG features.  Also, since you have to have the li‐
       brary  to run jjppeeggttooppnnmm, but not vice versa, ccjjppeegg may be more commonly
       available.

       On the other hand, ddjjppeegg does not use the NetPBM libraries to  generate
       its  output,  as all the NetPBM tools such as jjppeeggttooppnnmm do.  This means
       it is less likely to be consistent with all  the  other  programs  that
       deal with the NetPBM formats.  Also, the command syntax of jjppeeggttooppnnmm is
       consistent with that of the other Netpbm tools, unlike ddjjppeegg.


